{"slug":"BV.151.12","title":"Interval Numbers (Svarāntara Saṅkhyā)","printed_no":"21","sloka_from":152,"sloka_to":152,"summary":"Pathak ji's interval-number notation: twelve notes have eleven intervals; each number counts from the previous note, upward if written above the syllable, downward if below. Worked songs: 'Om Jai Jagdish Hare', the national anthem (on six holes, 2 is a whole finger, 1 a half hole) and 'Vande Mātaram' in rāga Des.","khanda":{"slug":"pradarshana","title":"Part Three: Performance"},"prev":{"slug":"BV.148.27","title":"An Introduction to Rāga Khamāj"},"next":null,"blocks":[{"id":"BV.151.12","kind":"heading","n":null,"level":2,"text":"Svarāntara saṅkhyā (interval numbers)"},{"id":"BV.152","kind":"sloka","n":152,"verse":"द्वादशानां स्वराणाम् तु मध्ये एकादशान्तराः।\nपूर्व वर्ति स्वरापेक्षा या संख्या सा विलिख्यते।१५२।","text":"Among the twelve notes there are eleven intervals. The number that is written down is the one reckoned from the note that comes before."},{"id":"BV.152.1","kind":"prose","n":null,"commentary":true,"text":"That is, komal and śuddha together, the notes number 12, but the intervals (svarāntara) between them are (11). Here the number written above or below the syllables of the song should be understood by counting from the note that comes before it; otherwise the difficulty will increase."},{"id":"BV.152.2","kind":"prose","n":null,"commentary":true,"text":"Following this same rule, some songs are presented here as examples. Suppose we want to play 'Om Jay Jagdīś Hare' starting from the Madhyama (Ma) note; then the number of Ma will be zero. Whatever note is used after it, its svarāntara saṅkhyā (interval number) will be written by counting from Ma, and then that same number will be played. A number that is below a syllable [is to be taken as that many steps down], and a number that is above must be understood as that many steps up."},{"id":"BV.152.3","kind":"heading","n":null,"level":4,"text":"Sthāī"},{"id":"BV.152.4","kind":"table","n":null,"table_html":"<table class=\"bv-table\"><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>1</td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>o</td><td>-m</td><td>jai</td><td>-</td><td>|</td><td>ja</td><td>ga</td><td>dī</td><td>-</td><td>|</td><td>śa</td><td>ha</td><td>re</td><td>-</td><td>|</td></tr><tr><td>0</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>svā</td><td>mī</td><td>jai</td><td>-</td><td>|</td><td>ja</td><td>ga</td><td>dī</td><td>-</td><td>|</td><td>śa</td><td>ha</td><td>re</td><td>-</td><td>|</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td><td>3</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>bha</td><td>-</td><td>kta</td><td>ja</td><td>|</td><td>no</td><td>-</td><td>ke</td><td>-</td><td>|</td><td>saṃ</td><td>-</td><td>ka</td><td>ṭa</td><td>|</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>4</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td>5</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>kṣa</td><td>ṇa</td><td>meṃ</td><td>-</td><td>|</td><td>dū</td><td>-</td><td>ra</td><td>ka</td><td>|</td><td>re</td><td>-</td><td>o</td><td>-m</td><td>|</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td>3</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>jai</td><td>-</td><td>ja</td><td>ga</td><td>|</td><td>dī</td><td>-</td><td>śa</td><td>ha</td><td>|</td><td>re</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>|</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>","caption":""},{"id":"BV.152.5","kind":"heading","n":null,"level":4,"text":"Antarā"},{"id":"BV.152.6","kind":"table","n":null,"table_html":"<table class=\"bv-table\"><tr><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>3</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>3</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>5</td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>jo</td><td>-</td><td>dhyā</td><td>-</td><td>ve</td><td>-</td><td>pha</td><td>la</td><td>pā</td><td>-</td><td>ve</td><td>-</td><td>du</td><td>kha</td><td>vi</td><td>na</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>5</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>se</td><td>-</td><td>ma</td><td>na</td><td>kā</td><td>-</td><td>svā</td><td>mī</td><td>|</td><td>du</td><td>kha</td><td>vi</td><td>na</td><td>|</td><td>se</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td></td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td></td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>4</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>-</td><td>ma</td><td>na</td><td>|</td><td>kā-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>|</td><td>su</td><td>kha</td><td>saṃ</td><td>-</td><td>pa</td><td>ti</td><td>gha</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>ra</td><td>ā</td><td>-</td><td>ve</td><td>-</td><td>ka</td><td>-</td><td>ṣṭa</td><td>mi</td><td>ṭe</td><td>-</td><td>ta</td><td>na</td><td>kā</td><td>-</td><td>|</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>2</td><td></td><td>4</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td><td>3</td><td></td></tr><tr><td></td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>o</td><td>-m</td><td>jai</td><td>-</td><td>ja</td><td>ga</td><td>|</td><td>dī</td><td>-</td><td>śa</td><td>ha</td><td>|</td><td>re</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>-</td><td>|</td></tr><tr><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>","caption":""},{"id":"BV.152.7","kind":"prose","n":null,"text":"All the remaining antarās will also be played in the same way."},{"id":"BV.152.8","kind":"prose","n":null,"text":"In the same way, if we write the national song in svarāntara lipi (interval notation), it will be as follows. If the flute has six holes, then, except for the third hole from the bottom, all the holes will be of two intervals each. So where the number (2) is given above a syllable, lift one finger fully; if the same number is given below the syllable, bring one finger fully down and close the hole. But where the number (1) is given, it will be understood as the sign to open or close half the hole. In the same way, the svarāntara saṅkhyā (interval number) should be used for all the holes on the basis of their proper values."},{"id":"BV.152.9","kind":"table","n":null,"table_html":"<ta